\DOC LOCAL_CMADD_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_CMADD_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes an inlined scaled-add-and-multiply (constant-multiple
plus addend) subroutine block in an {eventually} goal, advancing the symbolic
state variable by one step, re-expressing the state-dependent assumptions in
terms of the new state, and adding bounds and congruence facts for the freshly
computed result.

\DESCRIBE
{LOCAL_CMADD_4_TAC n [x1; x2; x3]} takes an {int} (in all observed traces the
same value) and a {string list} of three names identifying the result variable
and the two input operand variables of the block; the first name, {x1}, becomes
the newly introduced result variable appearing in the added assumptions. The
conclusion keeps its shape — an {eventually} postcondition applied to a state —
with the state argument replaced by the next-numbered state variable. Every
assumption mentioning the old state (program-counter, {bytes_loaded},
memory/register reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame) is discharged and
re-added in terms of the new state, with the frame extended by the memory
region written by the block, the program-counter assumption updated to a later
offset, and one memory-read assumption for the destination region added
equating it to {x1}. Two purely arithmetic assumptions about {x1} are also
added: an upper bound, and a modular congruence relating it to a fixed multiple
of one operand plus the other. Assumptions not referring to the state are
retained unchanged, though reordered.
{
   A ?- eventually ... (P s)
   =============================  LOCAL_CMADD_4_TAC n [x1; x2; x3]
   A' ?- eventually ... (P s')
}
\noindent where {s'} is the next-numbered state variable and {A'} is {A}
restated as described above, with the new assumptions about {x1}.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this same single-goal state-advancing behaviour on
{eventually} goals with large assumption lists.

\ENDDOC
